      "text": "To find \\( x \\) such that \\( \\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3 \\), we start by using the change of base formula for logarithms, which states that \\( \\log_a b = \\frac{\\log_c b}{\\log_c a} \\). We can rewrite the logarithms in terms of base 2:\n\n\\[ \\log_x 4 = \\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x} \\]\n\\[ \\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27} \\]\n\nSince \\( 4 = 2^2 \\), we have \\( \\log 4 = \\log (2^2) = 2\\log 2 \\). Also, since \\( 27 = 3^3 \\), we have \\( \\log 27 = \\log (3^3) = 3\\log 3 \\). Substituting these into the expressions, we get:\n\n\\[ \\log_x 4 = \\frac{2\\log 2}{\\log x} \\]\n\\[ \\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{3\\log 3} = \\frac{1}{3} \\]\n\nGiven that \\( \\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{1}{3} \\), we substitute this back into the first logarithm expression:\n\n\\[ \\frac{2\\log 2}{\\log x} = \\frac{1}{3} \\]\n\nTo solve for \\( \\log x \\), we cross-multiply:\n\n\\[ 2\\log 2 = \\frac{\\log x}{3} \\]\n\\[ 6\\log 2 = \\log x \\]\n\nSince \\( \\log 2 = \\log 2 \\), we recognize that \\( \\log 2 = 1 \\) implies \\( \\log 2 = 3 \\). But we should solve it step-by-step. Let's solve \\( \\log x = 6\\log 2 \\) using properties of logarithms again:\n\nSince \\( 6\\log 2 = \\log (2^6) \\):\n\n\\[ \\log x = 6 \\log 2 \\]\n\\[ \\log x = \\log (2^6) \\]\n\nTherefore, \\( x = 2^6 \\):\n\n\\[ x = 64 \\]\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}\n\\]",
